  "order": 8,
  "title": "Numbers instead of numerals",
  "summary": "Plain numbers for chords, the way session bands talk.",
  "minutes": 5,
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "Nashville number system",
      "body": "Many bands skip Roman numerals and use plain numbers: 1 to 7 for the chords of the key. That is the **Nashville number system**. A plain number means the key's own chord, so in C major 6 means A minor, just like vi.\n\n**Why it matters:** It is how most working bands call chords: \"it's a 1-5-6-4 in G\". Once you know the numbers, a chart works in any key the singer wants.",
      "exampleMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        64,
        65,
        67,
        69,
        71
      ],
      "labels": {
        "60": "1: C",
        "62": "2: Dm",
        "64": "3: Em",
        "65": "4: F",
        "67": "5: G",
        "69": "6: Am",
        "71": "7: B°"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "show",
      "title": "Numerals and numbers",
      "body": "I is 1, ii is 2, vi is 6, and so on.",
      "highlightMidi": [
        60,
        62,
        64,
        65,
        67,
        69,
        71
      ],
      "labels": {
